[38;5;252m[1m## What was found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One real divergence, in the class that happy-path testing cannot reach:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mcheckshift[0m's allocation-failure branch had been optimized out of existence.[22m LLVM recognizes [38;5;10mmalloc[0m/[38;5;10mfree[0m by name and had promoted the non-escaping 12-byte [38;5;10mComputeState[0m block to registers, deleting the allocation from the Rust [38;5;10m.so[0m entirely — 0 [38;5;10mmalloc[0m calls from Rust vs 1 from C under an [38;5;10mLD_PRELOAD[0m interposer, and no [38;5;10mcall malloc[0m in the disassembly. That made [38;5;10mstate == NULL[0m unreachable, so under allocation failure the C printed [38;5;10mError: Failed to allocate memory for state[0m and returned [38;5;10m-1[0m while the Rust returned a normal result. Fixed in [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m by routing both calls through [38;5;10m#[inline(never)][0m trampolines that load the function pointer via [38;5;10mread_volatile[0m, hiding the callee's identity from the optimizer. [38;5;10merr18b_allocator_call_parity[0m is the regression guard.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I also examined a second call-parity difference and deliberately left it: the C [38;5;10m.so[0m calls [38;5;10mmemcpy[0m for the small fixed-size struct copies that Rust inlines. Unlike [38;5;10mmalloc[0m, [38;5;10mmemcpy[0m cannot fail and no C branch is keyed on it, so no return value, state byte, or emitted byte changes. The one input class it could have affected — misaligned [38;5;10mint*[0m — is now covered by [38;5;10mERRORS.md[0m row 19.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
